 ## Subtopics
 
-### Multi-Plane Blending
-Uses explicit blending weights $\alpha_p$ for each plane [S1]. Choose when geometry noise is significant and 3D context is needed.
-
-### Volume Rendering Accumulation
-Uses density $\rho$ and discrete integration [S2, unofficial]. Choose when continuous volume representation is preferred.
+### Neural Point Cloud Rendering via Multi-Plane Projection (NPR-MPP)
+The primary method described. It projects features into a layered 3D volume to handle occlusions and noises, unlike NPG which projects to 2D. [S1]
+
+### 3D Convolutional Renderer
+Refers to the U-Net-like 3D CNN backbone used to predict multi-plane RGB images and blending weights. [S1]
+
+### MPP Network
+Another alias for the multi-plane projection network. [S1]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training takes approximately 41.5 hours per scene on a GeForce 1080 Ti [S1]. Inference time is not explicitly given but depends on the 3D CNN depth and resolution [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the voxel grid size and network depth. The input tensor is `[batch, 32, H, W, 8]` [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Effectively exploits information from neighboring pixels and depth, handling projection errors caused by geometry noise [S1]. Perceptual loss improves visual quality over L1/L2/SSIM [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires significant computational resources for training [S1]. Performance is tied to the quality of the input point cloud and voxelization [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering from sparse point clouds with significant noise or when high-quality perceptual results are needed [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time inference on low-power hardware is required, or when the input data is already dense and noise-free.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. Training takes on average 41.5 hours per scene on a GeForce 1080 Ti [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on ScanNet and Matterport 3D datasets [S1]. Produces more stable renderings compared to previous methods, especially near object boundaries [S1]. Performs reasonably well given relatively sparse point clouds [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Uses point clouds which are more scalable for larger scenes compared to DeepVoxel which relies on 3D volume [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to noisy and relatively sparse point clouds [S1]. Handles occlusions and noises appropriately by projecting to a layered 3D volume [S1]. Temporal coherence in image sequences [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a point cloud as input [S1]. Training time is significant [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering from raw 3D point clouds, especially in the presence of noise or sparsity, and when temporal stability is required [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When a dense 3D volume representation is already available and point clouds are not the primary input [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Multi-plane neural rendering was introduced in the paper "Neural Point Cloud Rendering via Multi-Plane Projection" [S1]. It was developed to improve the rendering quality of neural point clouds by using a 3D CNN to predict multi-plane RGB images and blending weights. The method was evaluated on the ScanNet and Matterport 3D datasets [S1]. An unofficial implementation is available on GitHub [S2, unofficial].
+Multi-plane Neural Rendering was proposed by Peng Dai, Yinda Zhang, Zhuwen Li, Shuaicheng Liu, and Bing Zeng from the University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Google Research, and Nuro Inc [S1]. It was published in CVPR 2020 [S1]. The method addresses the limitations of previous neural point-based graphics approaches like NPG, which suffer from visibility verification problems and noise interference due to direct 3D-to-2D projection [S1]. It builds on the idea of using learnable point features and view-dependent appearance [S1].
 
